FAQS = [
    ("Why has my hot water suddenly stopped?", "A complete loss of hot water can result from a power or gas supply problem, a tripped safety device, a failed element or thermostat, ignition trouble, a leak, or an ageing cylinder. Check only safe household controls you already understand. Do not remove covers or work around exposed wiring, gas or very hot water. Include the system type, approximate age, symptoms and any visible error code in your enquiry."),
    ("Should a leaking hot-water system be repaired or replaced?", "It depends on where the water is coming from. A valve, fitting or accessible component may be repairable. Water escaping from a corroded storage tank usually points towards replacement. Turn off the relevant supply only if you know how to do so safely, keep people away from electrical hazards, and arrange an assessment."),
    ("Which hot-water system suits Orange's cold climate?", "There is no single answer for every home. The right option depends on household size, peak demand, available energy, installation space, electrical capacity, budget, noise sensitivity and the performance data for the exact model. Heat pumps can work in cool climates, but model selection and placement matter."),
    ("Can I ask for advice before choosing a system?", "Yes. A useful enquiry includes the current unit, household size, usual shower pattern, available gas or electrical supply, roof or outdoor space, what is going wrong and whether your priorities are lower running cost, fast recovery, lower upfront cost or reduced emissions."),
    ("How long does replacement take?", "Timing varies with product availability, safe access, the condition of existing connections, whether the replacement is like-for-like, and whether electrical, plumbing, gas or structural changes are required. A provider should confirm scope and timing after reviewing the property rather than promising a universal timeframe."),
    ("Are NSW hot-water rebates guaranteed?", "No. Programs, eligibility rules, approved products and accredited-provider requirements can change. Check current NSW Government or energy-program information and ask the eventual provider to explain eligibility before relying on a rebate in your budget."),
    ("What information should I provide for a quote?", "Include the property suburb, system type and age if known, household size, symptoms or replacement goal, usual shower pattern and any safe-to-observe error code. Photographs of the model label and installation area can help when they can be taken without approaching hot water, gas, wiring or exposed equipment. Also state whether the priority is urgent restoration, lower running cost, improved recovery, electrification or planned replacement."),
    ("Can a heat pump work through an Orange winter?", "Many modern heat pumps are designed to operate in cool conditions, but performance varies materially by model. Compare the published operating range, recovery at low ambient temperature, tank capacity, backup-element behaviour, defrost approach and acoustic data. Positioning also matters: the unit needs suitable airflow, drainage, access and consideration for bedrooms and neighbours."),
    ("Why does my system seem noisier than before?", "A clear change in sound may indicate scale, sediment, a fan or compressor issue, ignition trouble, expansion or another developing fault. Some operating noise is normal, particularly for heat pumps and gas systems. Record when the sound occurs and, if safe, make a short recording. Do not remove panels or place hands near operating machinery to investigate."),
    ("Is a larger hot-water tank always better?", "No. A larger tank may provide more stored capacity, but it can increase purchase cost, space requirements and standing heat loss. Correct sizing considers peak demand, recovery rate, winter inlet temperature, tariff or control windows and future household changes. Ask the provider to explain the assumptions behind the recommended size."),
    ("What should be included in a replacement quote?", "A clear quote should identify the exact model and capacity and explain removal of the old unit, disposal, valves, drainage, pipework, electrical or gas work, base or mounting, access, commissioning, warranty responsibility and exclusions. Potential switchboard, roof, structural or gas-supply changes should be identified where they can reasonably be anticipated."),
    ("Do I need an emergency replacement immediately?", "Not every fault requires an immediate full replacement. A leaking storage vessel, serious corrosion or unsafe condition may make replacement likely, while a valve, thermostat, element or control fault may be repairable. Keep clear of hazards and seek an assessment. If there is smoke, sparking, a gas smell, uncontrolled hot water or water threatening electrical equipment, address the immediate safety risk first."),
]

def icon(name):
    paths = {
        "drop": '<path d="M12 2S5 10 5 15a7 7 0 0 0 14 0c0-5-7-13-7-13Z"/><path d="M9 16c.6 1.2 1.5 1.8 3 2"/>',
        "leaf": '<path d="M20 4C12 4 5 8 5 15c0 3 2 5 5 5 7 0 10-8 10-16Z"/><path d="M4 21c3-6 7-9 12-12"/>',
        "sun": '<circle cx="12" cy="12" r="4"/><path d="M12 2v2M12 20v2M4.9 4.9l1.4 1.4M17.7 17.7l1.4 1.4M2 12h2M20 12h2M4.9 19.1l1.4-1.4M17.7 6.3l1.4-1.4"/>',
        "tool": '<path d="M14.7 6.3a4 4 0 0 0-5-5L12 3.6 8.4 7.2 6.1 4.9a4 4 0 0 0 5 5L4 17a2 2 0 1 0 3 3l7.1-7.1a4 4 0 0 0 5-5l-2.3 2.3-3.6-3.6 1.5-1.3Z"/>',
    }
    return f'<svg class="icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true">{paths[name]}</svg>'

